Let's also consider that Plan A uses all 3 Reference units available, so if one is defective on arrival, no replacement. Plan B uses 2 Reference, leaving the third Reference unpurchased but available; if one fails, can we buy the third within budget? B base 5780 + 1800 = 7580 > 7200, but B resilience 6760 + 1800 = 8560. Short 380/1360. However, if we don't buy the spare Echo/logger/second battery initially, B base 5780 + 1800 = 7580, short 380. A budget increase of 380 is smaller than A's spare Reference increase (1580). So B is closer to being able to add a spare Reference. But still requires approval. This might make B more flexible. Hmm.

What if B base + third Reference = 7580; we could drop the second battery (not in base) and the spare Echo, still 7580. Short 380. A budget increase of 380 is plausible. A all-Reference + spare Reference = 8780, short 1580. So B is more resilient per dollar. But B's third Reference would be used at S2/S3 if one fails; it also leaves only 2 Ref initially. If one fails, buy the third. Good.

Could B use 3 Reference + Echo at S1 = 7580, over by 380; but then all sites Reference? That's all-Reference plus an Echo spare at S1, not needed. No.

